Z||000001||Terrybogin||11-20-1999||12:08 AM||||So, nobody has used lowering springs or they have and do not want to talk about it.<BR>Thanks guys, I will not ask for anything else.||||reg||1
Z||000002||olympic||11-20-1999||01:30 AM||||I've never lowered a car but if you go to the search function on this site and type in "lowering springs" you'll get a few older threads which may help you out....  The general consensus seems to be that lowering spindles are better because the factory geometry and suspension travel are retained.<p>[This message has been edited by olympic (edited 11-20-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||11-20-1999||08:14 PM||||My 88 is lowered with ACE Eibach lowering springs front and rear and my 85 in progress KitCar has Bell-Tech drop spindles front (evidently I got the last pair in existance) and Rayne Motorsports coil-overs in the rear||||reg||1
Z||000004||Racer 1||11-20-1999||09:50 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">I got suspension techniques lowering springs front and rear on my fiero. You can get them thru J.C.Whitney. My Fiero doesnt have that swamp buggy look in the back anymore.||||reg||14
Z||000005||superXmotorsports||11-20-1999||10:53 PM||||Terrybogin - looks like my post on clutch opinions!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I am getting ready to install a set of Suspension Techniques along with Koni adjustables in about 2 weeks.  I'll email you info about the results.||||reg||1
Z||000006||turbo86se||11-20-1999||11:38 PM||||Terrybogin,<BR>I've had great success with Suspension Techniques lowering springs on two of my Fieros. I had the lowering springs and KYB shocks and struts on my old 85 coupe, and I currently have lowering springs and KONI shocks and struts on my 86SE. It handles like it's on rails. Very smooth, now all I need is to get rid of the 14" wheels and tires! Don't worry about speedbumps, if you take your time, you'll be fine!||||reg||7
Z||000007||2birds||11-21-1999||12:56 AM||||My 86 GT has the JC Whitney/Suspension Techniques Springs, along with a complete polyurethane treatment.  About a two inch drop by eyeball measurement.  Looks much better, IMHO.  I also put the JC Whitney rear sway bar on.  The ride is a real kidney-buster, but it handles like a go-kart most of the time, at least on smooth roads.  I'm not real satisfied with the handling on rough or rutted interstates.  It seems to have a lot of bump steer, too.  I replaced all the ball joints with Moog parts, so the front camber is adjustable, and I have the greasable sway bar bushings.  The tie rod ends are original (162,000 miles), and the Monroe gasmatics seem okay even with 40,000 miles on them.  I think most of the rough ride is due to the poly bushings, but I would prefer variable rate springs if I was to do it again.  If you have bigger tires, you may rub the front inner fender liner at full lock.  I have 215/60/15 front and 225/60/15 rears.  Stock size cleared fine.  Also, be ready for problems getting it on the alignment or tire rack.  I have to drive it up in 2x4's just to get a jack under it.  One other thing, too.  I doubled my rear tire life, but I don't know if it was the poly bushings or the sway bar, since I did all of this at the same time.  Sorry to ramble, but maybe this will help.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Racer 1||11-21-1999||12:27 PM||||Dont get the poly bushing unless your racing. I also put them in my fiero feels like your riding in a red rider wagon. I wish I would have stuck with rubber bushings.||||reg||9
